Fail the load when the CD image cannot be parsed.

CDAccess_Open() (mednafen/cdrom/CDAccess.cpp) dispatches on extension for
.ccd/.pbp/.chd only and sends everything else to CDAccess_Image, the
CUE/TOC parser -- there is no sniffing and no rejection.  Hand the core an
image format it does not support and the parser finds no TRACK lines, so
ImageOpen() returns false at "if(FirstTrack > LastTrack)".

That return was discarded.  ImageOpen() signals failure by returning rather
than throwing, and CDIF_Open() does not validate either, so disc.cpp's
catch(std::exception&) never fired: a CDAccess_Image with zero tracks was
handed up as a successful load.  The frontend reported "game loaded" and the
Saturn BIOS, finding nothing bootable, dropped into its CD-player menu -- no
error logged anywhere, just the wrong screen.  (Observed with an .mds, which
this core does not support; its own libretro .info correctly advertises only
ccd|chd|cue|toc|m3u.)

Check the return and throw MDFN_Error, which the callers already handle.
Cleanup() runs first because a partial parse can have opened track streams
and the destructor does not run when a constructor throws.

--- a/mednafen/cdrom/CDAccess_Image.cpp
+++ b/mednafen/cdrom/CDAccess_Image.cpp
@@ -912,7 +912,25 @@
 {
    memset(Tracks, 0, sizeof(Tracks));
 
-   ImageOpen(path, image_memcache);
+   /* ImageOpen() reports a failed parse by *returning* false rather than
+      throwing, and its return used to be discarded here. CDAccess_Open() sends
+      every extension it does not recognise (anything but .ccd/.pbp/.chd) to
+      this CUE/TOC parser, so handing the core an image format it does not
+      support -- an .mds, say -- left a fully-constructed CDAccess_Image with
+      zero tracks. CDIF_Open() does not validate either, so the frontend was
+      told the game had loaded and the Saturn BIOS, finding nothing bootable,
+      dropped into its CD-player menu: no error anywhere, just the wrong screen.
+
+      Turn it into the exception the callers already expect -- disc.cpp wraps
+      the load in catch(std::exception&) and MDFN_Error derives from it -- so an
+      unreadable image fails the load cleanly. Cleanup() first: a partial parse
+      can already have opened track streams, and ~CDAccess_Image() does not run
+      when the constructor throws. */
+   if(!ImageOpen(path, image_memcache))
+   {
+      Cleanup();
+      throw(MDFN_Error(0, _("Could not parse CD image \"%s\": unsupported format, or the file is corrupt."), path.c_str()));
+   }
 }
 
 CDAccess_Image::~CDAccess_Image()
